  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates a trapped-ion quantum system and how internal ion states are linked to collective motion. Overall, it combines an energy-level picture with a schematic of ions in a trap and examples of different vibrational patterns.\n\nThe left energy diagram shows three relevant states: a ground state, an excited state, and a qubit state labeled with a splitting around ω0. Colored arrows indicate laser-driven transitions, suggesting that light can couple the qubit levels to the excited state and also address motional sidebands separated by ωk. This conveys the basic mechanism used to control qubit states through optical excitation while being sensitive to the ions’ shared motion.\n\nThe lower schematic shows several ions confined in a trap potential and aligned in a chain by the balance of trapping forces and Coulomb repulsion. Each ion contains the two-level qubit structure, and the chain is illuminated by both a global laser beam and an individual addressing beam. Above the chain, a harmonic ladder labeled as a motional mode represents quantized collective vibration.\n\nThe right panel depicts several possible normal modes of the chain, with arrows showing different ions moving in different directions, emphasizing that the ions can oscillate collectively in distinct spatial patterns.",
